# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (file "HoareLogic Exercises.pdf"), problem 3 d), with its solution sheet.
# The exercise asks for the precondition. The precondition given here is the answer.
id: conditional-even
title: A conditional that makes x even
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 3 d
tags: conditional, wp, even, odd

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$: first the weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the
  conditional. Then use the consequence rule to check that the
  precondition $P$ implies it.

given:
  Condition B: $x > 0$

pre: (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
post: even(x)

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if (x > 0) then {
    {R1}
    x := x + 1        // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    x := z            // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch x := x + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment
  expression ($x + 1$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, Q)
  = even(x)[x <- x + 1]
  = even(x + 1)         // x is replaced by x + 1
  = odd(x)              // x + 1 is even exactly when x is odd
set: R1 = odd(x)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch x := z
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment
  expression ($z$) in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := z, Q)
  = even(x)[x <- z]
  = even(z)             // x is replaced by z
set: R2 = even(z)

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x > 0$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x > 0 : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x > 0) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if (x > 0) then x := x + 1 else x := z, Q)
  = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (!(x > 0) ==> even(z))    // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))      // !(x > 0) is x <= 0
set: R3 = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
text:
  We stop here. The formula reads as two cases: if $x > 0$, then $x$ must
  be odd; otherwise $z$ must be even.

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z)) ==> (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
why:
  $P$ and $R3$ are the same formula, and every formula implies itself.
result: The triple is valid, and $P$ is the weakest precondition.

summary:
  When $x > 0$, the program adds $1$ to $x$, so $x$ must be odd for the
  result to be even. Otherwise it copies $z$ into $x$, so $z$ must be
  even.
